; The last two expressions that answered a type matching everything.
;
; A minted wildcard is `tyCompat`-compatible with every type, so any
; form that answers one launders its value into whatever the context
; wants. `checkApp` was the big one and closed on 2026-08-17 (563);
; these two are the rest of the set, found by asking which other
; expression forms mint a wildcard rather than answering what they
; compute.
;
; 1. `handle` threw its body's type away and answered `mkSilentWild`.
;    A handler runs an operation and RESUMES into the body - it does
;    not replace what the body answers - so the form's value is the
;    body's value and its type is the body's type. Measured:
;
;      (strLen (handle (useIt 3) (Tick) (lambda (v) (+ v 27))))
;
;    where `useIt` is `(-> Int Int)` checked clean and exited 139.
;
; 2. An `else`-less `cond` at an ARROW type. `tyZeroInhabits` permitted
;    arrows, lists and pointers deliberately - the corpus population
;    was 0 and widening a refusal on no evidence is how a checker
;    acquires rules nobody can justify later. The evidence arrived, and
;    the arrow is the loudest of the three because the fall-through 0
;    is a null CLOSURE:
;
;      (:: f (-> Int (-> Int Int)))
;      (fn (f n) (cond ((> n 5) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)))))
;      ((f 0) 3)     ->  check OK, exit 133
;
;    A list and a pointer are the same null one step quieter. All three
;    are refused now; the unit tuple still is not, because 0 does
;    inhabit it. Corpus population is still 0, so this cost nothing.

(import Str)

(effect Tick
  (bump :: (-> Int Int)))

(:: useIt (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(Tick)
(fn (useIt n) (bump n))

; Refused: the handle answers `Int`, which `strLen` does not take.
(:: viaHandle (-> Int Int))
(fn (viaHandle n)
  (strLen (handle (useIt n) (Tick) (lambda (v) (+ v 27)))))

; Refused: nothing answers when n is 0, and a null closure is not a
; function.
(:: viaArrow (-> Int (-> Int Int)))
(fn (viaArrow n)
  (cond ((> n 5) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)))))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
